Design Document

LinearEquation class

Instance Variables:
private int x1 - This variable represents the x-coordinate of the first point
private int y1 - y-coordinate of the first point
private int x2 - x-coordinate of the second point
private int y2 - y-coordinate of the second point

Constructor
	public LinearEquation(int x1, int y1, int x2, int y2)

Methods

public String calculateSlope()
This method uses the coordinates to find the rise and run of the slope. Then I use conditions to format the slope into either an integer or a fraction.

public double calculateYIntercept()
This method finds the y-intercept of the line with the slope and one point using the formula: y=mx+b. Returns the y-intercept.

public String createLinearEquation()
This method uses the calculateSlope() and calculateYIntercept() methods to create an equation in slope intercept form. There will be conditions to format the y-intercept and the “-1” and “1” slopes correctly into the equation.

public String toString()
This method will display all relevant information about this instance including:
X and Y coordinate information
Slope Intercept Form equation
Distance between the two points
Slope of line
Y-intercept

public double calculateThirdPoint(double x3)
This method will use x3 provided by the user. X3 will be multiplied by the slope and the y-intercept will be added to the product to find the y-coordinate. Returns the y-coordinate.

public double calculateDistance()
This method will calculate the distance between the first two points provided by the user and return the result.

EquationRunner.java

Step 1: Ask user to enter first coordinate point
Step 2: Ask user to enter second coordinate point
Step 3: Store each of the two points in a variable as a string.
Step 4: Use the substring method to pick out the individual x and y-coordinates and store them in variables.
Step 4: Parse these coordinates into integers.
Step 5: Make an instance of the LinearEquation class using the constructor method.
Step 6: Use println and the object’s name in the parameter to print out all the information of the points.
Step 7: Ask the user for a third x-coordinate
Step 8: Call on the calculateThirdPoint method and store the result in a string variable.
Step 9: Print out the solved point.
